# Instagram through HasData

Three tools. Two are keyed on `handle`, the third on the URL of a single post. `hasdata_instagram_profile_getInstagramProfile` returns the account, and `hasdata_instagram_posts_getInstagramPosts` returns its feed with `limit` and `nextPageToken`.

The profile already embeds `latestPosts`, so a question about the account and its recent work is one call rather than two.

## What a post does not carry

A post holds `id`, `shortcode`, `caption`, `type`, `productType`, `url`, `displayUrl`, `images`, `dimensionsWidth`, `dimensionsHeight`, `ownerUsername`, `ownerId` and `hasdataLink`. It also carries `likesCount`, `commentsCount`, `timestamp` and `publishDate`, so the feed alone can rank posts by engagement and filter them by date. Do not spend a call on `hasdata_instagram_comments_getInstagramComments` to collect numbers the feed already returned; reach for it when the comment thread itself is wanted. `mentions` and `hashtags` appear only when the caption has them, `taggedUsers` lists accounts tagged in the media, and a carousel puts its members in `childPosts`.

`mentions` appears on many posts and `hashtags` on fewer, so both are optional. Parsing the caption yourself finds the same tags, which is the reliable route when coverage matters.

## Twelve is the ceiling

One call returns twelve posts. `limit` above that still returns twelve, so a larger feed comes from following `nextPageToken` rather than from asking for more. The first page also repeats the twelve the profile already gave you, which makes the profile call the cheaper start.

## Public, private and broken

A private account answers with `private: true` and little else. Branch on that flag before reading anything else, because the rest of the payload is thin by design rather than missing by accident.

A handle that does not resolve comes back with `isError` true and a 400. Read the error text rather than only the flag, because the same flag carries a 401 for a bad key and `MCP error -32602` for a schema violation, and reporting a key problem as a missing account sends the reader after the wrong bug.

`id`, `username`, `followersCount`, `followsCount`, `profilePicUrl` and `fbid` are always there. `fullName`, `biography`, `verified`, `bioLinks` and `latestPosts` arrive on public accounts only, so read them defensively.

Each successful call spends credits from the connected account, and paging the feed is a call per page.
